ultramodern: let the host hold the clock, and say when the game is done with a frame

A game does a frame's work and then waits for the video interface to say the
picture was drawn. `vi_thread_func` is what says so, sixty times a second,
because that is what a television does -- and for a player that is exactly
right.

It is wrong for everything that is not a player. Something driving the game
from outside -- a script, or something learning to play it -- wants the game to
advance when it is asked to and then stop dead, and wants it to happen as fast
as the machine manages rather than at the speed of a television. Sixty frames a
second is also the ceiling on how much of the game such a thing can see, which
for anything that learns by playing is the whole cost of the exercise.

So the retrace comes off the clock. `set_host_paced_retraces` leaves the VI
thread with nothing to do and `host_retrace` does what it did, from whichever
thread asks; the interrupt is identical, only its timing is somebody else's.

The half that is not obvious is knowing when the game has finished reacting.
A frame is not over when the picture was handed over -- the game is still
running code the retrace set off, on threads of its own. It is over when
nothing is left, and that is three things at once: a thread is parked waiting
for the hardware with nothing runnable above it, no message has been sent that
has not been delivered, and no task the game gave the signal processor is still
outstanding. `wait_until_quiescent` checks all three rather than waiting out a
duration, and an epoch counts the times the game has gone quiet so that a
caller can tell quiet-about-this-frame from quiet-about-the-one-before.

Two details it took a stuck console to learn. A task handed to the signal
processor is answered on another thread long after the game asked, so the
message that ends it can arrive after everything else has gone quiet -- hence
the count of outstanding work, incremented where the task is submitted rather
than where it is run. And "nothing runnable" has to mean nothing runnable
*above the parked thread*: the idle thread of every game sits in the running
queue at priority zero behind whoever swapped away from it, for the whole life
of the game, so a test for an empty queue calls every frame unfinished.

