+// --- A console somebody else holds the clock for -----------------------------
+//
+// Nothing in a game asks for a frame. It does a frame's work and then waits for
+// the video interface to say the picture was drawn, and the interface says so
+// sixty times a second because that is what a television does. `vi_thread_func`
+// is that television, and it is what keeps a game running at the speed a player
+// sees it at.
+//
+// A player is not the only thing that can be at the other end of a game. A
+// script driving it, or something learning to play it, wants the opposite deal:
+// the game should advance exactly when it is asked to and then stop dead, so
+// that whatever reads the result reads a console with nothing left to do rather
+// than one halfway through a frame. It is also the only way to run faster than
+// sixty frames a second, which is usually the reason for asking.
+//
+// So the retrace can be taken off the clock and given to the host.
+// `host_retrace` is one interrupt of the video interface; `wait_until_quiescent`
+// is the other half of it, and returns once every thread of the game is parked
+// waiting for the hardware again and the hardware has nothing outstanding. That
+// is the console's own account of "done with that frame", and it is checked
+// rather than waited out.
